{
  "where_we_lag": {
    "headline": {
      "text": "In Helsinki, the crude share of patients with a recorded MDT meeting is 0.1% (95% CI 0.0–0.4; n=1/1486), whose 95% confidence interval excludes the 6.0% (893/14873) national figure.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "cty:mdt_recorded:Helsingin_kaupunki",
        "nat:mdt_recorded"
      ]
    },
    "details": [
      {
        "text": "Across overlapping treatment categories where a patient can have several, Helsinki's crude curative treatment share is 33.0% (95% CI 30.7–35.5; n=491/1486), whose 95% confidence interval excludes and lies above the 27.0% (4009/14873) national share, while crude life-prolonging treatment is 30.8% (95% CI 28.5–33.1; n=457/1486), whose 95% confidence interval includes the 30.4% (4522/14873) national value.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "cty:curative:Helsingin_kaupunki",
          "nat:curative",
          "cty:life_prolonging:Helsingin_kaupunki",
          "nat:life_prolonging"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "In these overlapping treatment categories, Helsinki's crude symptomatic treatment share is 34.5% (95% CI 32.1–37.0; n=513/1486), whose 95% confidence interval excludes and lies below the 39.5% (5871/14873) national figure, while the crude share with no treatment record within 12 months is 29.1% (95% CI 26.8–31.4; n=432/1486), whose 95% confidence interval includes 27.5% (4089/14873) nationally.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "cty:symptomatic:Helsingin_kaupunki",
          "nat:symptomatic",
          "cty:no_treatment_record:Helsingin_kaupunki",
          "nat:no_treatment_record"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "In the national registry report, MDT codes measure recording practice rather than care because HILMO entries come only from direct patient contacts, and no treatment record within 12 months mixes untreated patients with unrecorded treatment.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "bs:coverage:MDT",
          "q:39e6ba43a9",
          "bs:coverage:Treatment_start"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Nationally, preliminary unpublished figures show lung cancer takes 155 days on average from first symptoms to first treatment in Finland versus 107 days in Sweden and 67 days in Denmark, comprising 56 days of patient delay, 41 days in primary care, and 58 days in specialist care.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "mot:total_fi",
          "mot:total_se",
          "mot:total_dk",
          "mot:patient",
          "mot:primary",
          "mot:specialist"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "how_sure_are_we": {
    "headline": {
      "text": "County treatment shares are crude, are not age-adjusted, and cannot be stage-adjusted because stage at diagnosis is missing for about 48% of patients in the national report.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "bs:coverage:Diagnosis",
        "q:7885b0974f",
        "k16:curative:men:national"
      ]
    },
    "details": [
      {
        "text": "Helsinki's crude curative share of 33.0% (95% CI 30.7–35.5; n=491/1486) has separate limitations: county table shares are not age-adjusted, and independently they cannot be stage-adjusted because stage at diagnosis is missing for about 48% of patients.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "cty:curative:Helsingin_kaupunki",
          "bs:coverage:Diagnosis",
          "q:7885b0974f"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Age-adjusted values exist only from Kuva 16 for curative treatment by sex, where the national reference lines digitised from the figure are 26.7% for men and 26.9% for women at ±0.5 percentage points precision, and without a county Kuva 16 figure we cannot test whether Helsinki's crude curative difference holds after age adjustment.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "k16:curative:men:national",
          "k16:curative:women:national"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "As a data-quality signal, Helsinki's crude share with no treatment record within 12 months is 29.1% (95% CI 26.8–31.4; n=432/1486), whose 95% confidence interval includes the 27.5% (4089/14873) national value, while a 2019 Lääkärilehti journal article found HILMO diagnoses match the Cancer Registry for about 90% of most cancer types, concerning diagnosis concordance only.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "cty:no_treatment_record:Helsingin_kaupunki",
          "nat:no_treatment_record",
          "lit:hilmo_90"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Public data do not measure symptoms or referrals, primary care visits are not included in the report's data and occupational health is not mentioned, imaging appears only indirectly as clinical-only diagnosis in about 20% of patients without wait times, and specialist visit (93.6%) and treatment records within 12 months lack start dates.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "bs:coverage:Symptoms",
          "bs:coverage:Referral",
          "bs:coverage:Primary_care_occupational_health",
          "q:323de0d90d",
          "bs:coverage:Imaging",
          "q:5eb155decc",
          "bs:coverage:First_specialist_visit",
          "bs:coverage:Treatment_start"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "what_to_do_first": {
    "headline": {
      "text": "The team recommends publishing county recording completeness for stage, drug treatment, and MDT coding, reporting access times under THL 21/2016 definitions with handover alerts, and standardising referrals and coordination.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "rec:1",
        "rec:2",
        "rec:3",
        "thl:21_2016"
      ]
    },
    "details": [
      {
        "text": "Given Helsinki's crude 0.1% (95% CI 0.0–0.4; n=1/1486) MDT-recorded share versus 6.0% (893/14873) nationally and about 48% missing stage nationally, the team recommends publishing county recording completeness for stage, drug treatment, and MDT coding while STM roadmap measure 3.2.1 plans a national quality registry decree by 2026–2028 targeting 90% coverage including stage by 2029–2031.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "rec:3",
          "cty:mdt_recorded:Helsingin_kaupunki",
          "nat:mdt_recorded",
          "bs:coverage:Diagnosis",
          "q:950a2f797b",
          "q:f07a34a300",
          "q:4a856bd695",
          "q:f540c706f8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Because Health Care Act §52 sets no legal maximum time for a cancer suspicion and Finland lacks national access-time monitoring, the team recommends reporting referral-to-treatment access times under THL Ohjaus 21/2016 definitions (for 16 cancers) and flagging stalled handovers while STM roadmap measure 3.1.2 plans national pathways and access times by 2026–2028 and 2029–2031.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "rec:1",
          "law:no_max",
          "q:4e702a20fe",
          "thl:21_2016",
          "q:ed87906241",
          "q:def306014c",
          "q:b326e35410"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The team recommends adopting a standard referral template and persistent-symptoms rule modelled on the KYS regional pathway document—which directs primary care to refer persistent unexplained symptoms for a pulmonologist assessment even after a normal X-ray—and naming a diagnostic-phase contact person, backed by mentor insight that weak referrals receive lower urgency ratings.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "rec:2",
          "reg:kys_template",
          "reg:kys_persistent",
          "reg:only_kys_xray",
          "reg:named_contact",
          "mentor:kaisa_referral"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "While the Filha National Lung Cancer Programme's diagnostics recommendation with primary-care referral criteria and specialist time limits is to be carried out before the 2030 mid-term evaluation, and STM roadmap measures 2.5.2 and 1.2.3 plan standardised referral criteria and care-coordinator guidance by 2026–2028 for county adoption by 2029–2031, the team recommends acting now.",
        "claim_ids": [
          "rec:1",
          "rec:2",
          "q:29b792498e",
          "q:8f358a9de6",
          "q:614febebf2",
          "q:432c0e51d6",
          "q:37bb4c3b38",
          "q:c61439b30f",
          "q:fc81032a80",
          "q:abcbb06ae3"
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
